Function Declaration (Prototype)
What is Function Declaration?
Calling a function that is defined later in the code requires compulsory function declaration.
Function declaration specifies:

Name of the function
Number of arguments
Type of arguments
Order of arguments
Return type


The first line of the function definition is called the function declarator. Function Declarator and Function Declaration should match.


Structure
void test(5, 4);           → function declaration (wrong: values in declaration)
void test(int a, float b); → function declarator  ✓
{ ... }                    → function definition
test();                    → function call

Example — Without Declaration (CE)
cvoid main() {
    test();       // CE: test() defined after
}
void test() {
    printf("Happy");
}

Example — With Declaration (Works)
cvoid main() {
    void test();  // function declaration
    test();
}
void test() {
    printf("Happy");
}
// O/P: Happy

Example — With Parameters
cvoid main() {
    test(4, 5.1);
}
void test(int a, float f) {
    printf("%d %f", a, f);
}
// CE (no declaration) — need to add: void test(int, float); before call
Correct version:
cvoid main() {
    void test(int a, float b);   // declaration
    void test(4, 5.1);
}
void test(int a, float f) {
    printf("%d %f", a, f);
}
// O/P: 4 5.1
